	faithHeading="Faith and the Bible in Romania"
	faithBodyHtml={`<p>Romania counted <strong>19,053,815 residents</strong> in its 2021 census. Of those who stated a religion, <strong>85.3% were Orthodox</strong> (about 14 million people, down from over 16.3 million in 2011), 4.5% Roman Catholic and 2.5% Pentecostal &mdash; the only church to grow. About 2.7 million people, close to 15% of the population, did not declare a religion.</p><p>The Bible is old here. The Bucharest Bible of 1688 gave Romanians the whole Scripture in their own language, and in 1921 Dumitru Cornilescu&rsquo;s version made it readable in everyday speech for a new generation of Orthodox and Evangelical readers alike.</p><p>The liturgy and the icon are loved, and the Scriptures are heard on Sunday. The invitation of Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade; is a personal one: read the same Word in clear Romanian, and meet in it a Father.</p>`}
